//! lotus/stereo_smooth_runs.rye — ALES153: the run-length smoother carried into stereo, TWO INDEPENDENT HYSTERESIS HOLDS — the smoothing ALES95 named as a later rung,
//! now read across both speakers. ALES101 named `smooth_runs`: absorb every run shorter than a minimum into what surrounds it, so a one-frame tick inside a pause no
//! longer splits it and a spurious quiet frame inside speech no longer spares a click — the decisive robustness move that turns a per-frame reader into a usable
//! segmenter. That is a mono rewrite. Carried into stereo it meets NO desync wall, unlike the silence edits (ALES148–152): `smooth_runs` is READ-ONLY over the labels —
//! it rewrites a run-length sequence and touches no PCM, changing no clip length — so the two channels never fall out of balance. Its honest stereo shape is the
//! ANALYSIS-CORNER one ALES146 and ALES147 took: run the proven mono reader on EACH channel independently, keeping the two results apart in a StereoSegments.
//!
//! The rule, stated once: smooth `in.left` into `out.left` and `in.right` into `out.right` through ALES101's own `smooth_runs`, with the one shared `min_run`. The two
//! channels flicker independently — ALES147 proved their segmentations differ in count and boundary — so they smooth independently: a tick on the left is absorbed by the
//! left's neighbours, never the right's. Each channel inherits every one of ALES101's laws, because each channel IS a mono smooth.
//!
//! The crux — the STEREO SMOOTH LAW: each channel's smoothed runs equal ALES101's mono `smooth_runs` on that channel, run for run. This rung invents no new rewrite and
//! no new fault; it composes the proven mono smoother twice, once per side, and its whole content is that the two sides stay apart (a left blip smoothed by the left, a
//! right blip by the right) rather than one shared list — the same per-channel independence the stereo segmenter proved for the runs this smooths.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES153.
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-055925_fill-ales153-lotus-stereo-smooth-runs.md
//! Stands on: lotus/smooth_runs.rye (ALES101) — the mono run-length smoother, run once per channel, its `smooth_runs` and `SmoothError` reused whole; lotus/stereo_segment.rye
//! (ALES147) — the stereo voice-activity segmenter, whose `StereoSegments{left, right}` this reads and rewrites (the two channels' runs kept apart); lotus/segment.rye
//! (ALES95) — the mono `Segments` and `max_segments`; lotus/voiced.rye (ALES94) — the Verdict the runs carry.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE — software only, purely LOCAL, and READ-ONLY over the LABELS. A bounded in-process rewrite of two Segments (left, right) on one bench, siloed to lotus/,
//! touching no PCM on either speaker. A causal, left-to-right minimum-duration hold per channel: it cannot rescue a too-short OPENING run on either side and does not do
//! the symmetric two-sided smoothing or the adaptive floors a production VAD would — those stay later rungs. Not a transcript, not a diarization; no real sample rate, no
//! network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real speaker.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/stereo_smooth_runs.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const segment = @import("segment.rye");
const voiced = @import("voiced.rye");
const stereo_segment = @import("stereo_segment.rye");
const smooth_runs = @import("smooth_runs.rye");

pub const stereo_smooth_runs_version = "20260815.055925";

/// The stereo smoother's only fault is ALES101's OWN `SmoothError` (the honest, unreachable `SegmentsFull` — smoothing only ever merges runs, so a smoothed list can
/// never grow past `max_segments`) reused by name. The stereo lift invents none of its own.
pub const StereoSmoothError = smooth_runs.SmoothError;

/// Smooth BOTH channels of `in` into `out` by absorbing every run shorter than `min_run` samples into the run it extends, per channel, keeping each channel's tiling exact
/// and coalesced. READ-ONLY over the labels — not one PCM sample is read or written on either speaker; this rewrites two run-length sequences, nothing else. Runs ALES101's
/// proven mono `smooth_runs` on `in.left → out.left`, then on `in.right → out.right`, with the one shared `min_run`. Each channel is smoothed on its OWN runs, so the two
/// results stay apart. `min_run = 0` is the identity on both; an empty channel yields an empty side; a single-run channel is unchanged.
pub fn smooth_runs_stereo(
    in: *const stereo_segment.StereoSegments,
    out: *stereo_segment.StereoSegments,
    min_run: u32,
) StereoSmoothError!void {
    // invariant: each side of the input is a well-formed run list the mono smoother can trust — no more than the bound. The per-run tiling/coalesced contract is asserted
    // inside ALES101's own `smooth_runs`, so this seam names only the ceiling both sides share.
    assert(in.left.len <= segment.max_segments and in.right.len <= segment.max_segments);

    // Smooth each channel through ALES101's proven mono smoother, into its own side of `out`. Read-only: `smooth_runs` writes no sample and only ever merges runs, so
    // neither call can grow past the bound. The two channels are smoothed apart — a short excursion on one side is absorbed only by that side's neighbours.
    try smooth_runs.smooth_runs(&in.left, &out.left, min_run);
    try smooth_runs.smooth_runs(&in.right, &out.right, min_run);

    // invariant: smoothing never grows either channel's run count — it only ever merges. This is ALES101's own postcondition, restated here per side for the stereo seam.
    assert(out.left.len <= in.left.len and out.right.len <= in.right.len);
    // invariant: neither side exceeds the run-list ceiling — the mono smoother checks it at every push.
    assert(out.left.len <= segment.max_segments and out.right.len <= segment.max_segments);
}

// --- selftest: THE STEREO SMOOTH LAW — each channel's smoothed runs equal ALES101's mono smooth_runs on that channel, run for run, across a grid of min_run values and
//     several per-channel input shapes (the crux); THE INDEPENDENCE LAW — a left blip and a clean right smooth apart, identical channels smooth identically; THE
//     WELL-FORMED LAW — each output tiles the same span, is coalesced, and holds min_run past the first, per channel; THE DEGENERATE LAW — min_run 0 the identity on both,
//     an empty channel empty, a single run unchanged, a min_run past a channel's span coalescing it to one ------------------------------------------------------------

fn same(a: *const segment.Segments, b: *const segment.Segments) bool {
    if (a.len != b.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < a.len) : (i += 1) {
        if (a.items[i].verdict != b.items[i].verdict) return false;
        if (a.items[i].start != b.items[i].start) return false;
        if (a.items[i].count != b.items[i].count) return false;
    }
    return true;
}

/// The summed length of every run in a sequence — the span it tiles.
fn total_count(seq: *const segment.Segments) u32 {
    var sum: u32 = 0;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < seq.len) : (i += 1) sum += seq.items[i].count;
    return sum;
}

/// Verify the three data laws on one smoothed channel against its source: coverage (same first start and total length), coalesced (no adjacent shared verdict), and the
/// hold (every run past the first at least min_run). An independent reference to ALES101's own well_smoothed, checked here per channel.
fn well_smoothed(out: *const segment.Segments, in: *const segment.Segments, min_run: u32) bool {
    if (in.len == 0) return out.len == 0;
    if (out.len == 0 or out.len > in.len) return false;
    if (out.items[0].start != in.items[0].start) return false;
    var covered: u32 = 0;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < out.len) : (i += 1) {
        if (out.items[i].start != out.items[0].start + covered) return false; // tiles contiguously
        if (out.items[i].count == 0) return false;
        if (i > 0 and out.items[i].verdict == out.items[i - 1].verdict) return false; // coalesced
        if (i > 0 and out.items[i].count < min_run) return false; // hold, first exempt
        covered += out.items[i].count;
    }
    return covered == total_count(in);
}

/// Build a Segments by hand from a list of (verdict, count) pairs tiling from start 0 — a small independent way to hand a known label sequence to the smoother.
fn build(out: *segment.Segments, verdicts: []const voiced.Verdict, counts: []const u32) void {
    out.len = 0;
    var start: u32 = 0;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < verdicts.len) : (i += 1) {
        out.items[out.len] = .{ .verdict = verdicts[i], .start = start, .count = counts[i] };
        out.len += 1;
        start += counts[i];
    }
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    const V = voiced.Verdict;

    // 1. THE STEREO SMOOTH LAW — over a grid of min_run values, each channel's smoothed runs equal ALES101's mono smooth_runs on that channel, run for run. The two
    //    channels carry DIFFERENT flicker on purpose: the left a blip inside a pause, the right a clean two-region take.
    {
        const mins = [_]u32{ 0, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 20, 100, 1000 };
        var mi: u32 = 0;
        while (mi < mins.len) : (mi += 1) {
            const m = mins[mi];
            var in = stereo_segment.StereoSegments{};
            build(&in.left, &[_]V{ .silent, .voiced, .silent, .unvoiced }, &[_]u32{ 100, 2, 100, 40 });
            build(&in.right, &[_]V{ .voiced, .silent }, &[_]u32{ 80, 80 });
            var got = stereo_segment.StereoSegments{};
            try smooth_runs_stereo(&in, &got, m);

            var want_l = segment.Segments{};
            try smooth_runs.smooth_runs(&in.left, &want_l, m);
            var want_r = segment.Segments{};
            try smooth_runs.smooth_runs(&in.right, &want_r, m);
            if (!same(&got.left, &want_l)) return error.LeftNotMonoSmoothed;
            if (!same(&got.right, &want_r)) return error.RightNotMonoSmoothed;
            if (!well_smoothed(&got.left, &in.left, m)) return error.LeftNotWellSmoothed;
            if (!well_smoothed(&got.right, &in.right, m)) return error.RightNotWellSmoothed;
        }
    }

    // 2. THE INDEPENDENCE LAW — a left carrying a short blip smooths to fewer runs while a clean right is untouched; the left's absorption never touches the right. With
    //    min_run 10: the left silent(100)·voiced(2)·silent(100) coalesces to one silent(202), the right voiced(80)·silent(80) stays two.
    {
        var in = stereo_segment.StereoSegments{};
        build(&in.left, &[_]V{ .silent, .voiced, .silent }, &[_]u32{ 100, 2, 100 });
        build(&in.right, &[_]V{ .voiced, .silent }, &[_]u32{ 80, 80 });
        var got = stereo_segment.StereoSegments{};
        try smooth_runs_stereo(&in, &got, 10);
        if (got.left.len != 1 or got.left.items[0].verdict != .silent or got.left.items[0].count != 202) return error.IndependenceLeftWrong;
        if (got.right.len != 2 or got.right.items[0].verdict != .voiced or got.right.items[1].count != 80) return error.IndependenceRightWrong;
    }

    // 3. IDENTICAL CHANNELS smooth identically — a master with the same runs on both sides yields the same smoothed runs on both.
    {
        var in = stereo_segment.StereoSegments{};
        build(&in.left, &[_]V{ .voiced, .silent, .voiced, .silent, .unvoiced }, &[_]u32{ 40, 3, 5, 50, 4 });
        build(&in.right, &[_]V{ .voiced, .silent, .voiced, .silent, .unvoiced }, &[_]u32{ 40, 3, 5, 50, 4 });
        var got = stereo_segment.StereoSegments{};
        try smooth_runs_stereo(&in, &got, 8);
        if (!same(&got.left, &got.right)) return error.IdenticalNotIdentical;
    }

    // 4. THE DEGENERATE LAW — min_run 0 is the identity on both channels; an empty channel yields an empty side; a single-run channel is unchanged.
    {
        var in = stereo_segment.StereoSegments{};
        build(&in.left, &[_]V{ .silent, .voiced, .unvoiced }, &[_]u32{ 12, 20, 16 });
        // right stays empty (a channel with no runs)
        var got = stereo_segment.StereoSegments{};
        try smooth_runs_stereo(&in, &got, 0);
        if (!same(&got.left, &in.left)) return error.IdentityLeftChanged;
        if (got.right.len != 0) return error.EmptyRightNotEmpty;

        var one = stereo_segment.StereoSegments{};
        build(&one.left, &[_]V{.voiced}, &[_]u32{3});
        build(&one.right, &[_]V{.silent}, &[_]u32{7});
        var got1 = stereo_segment.StereoSegments{};
        try smooth_runs_stereo(&one, &got1, 10);
        if (got1.left.len != 1 or got1.left.items[0].count != 3) return error.SingleLeftChanged;
        if (got1.right.len != 1 or got1.right.items[0].count != 7) return error.SingleRightChanged;
    }

    // 5. A MIN_RUN PAST A CHANNEL'S WHOLE SPAN coalesces that channel to one run carrying its FIRST verdict, independent of the other channel's span.
    {
        var in = stereo_segment.StereoSegments{};
        build(&in.left, &[_]V{ .silent, .voiced, .unvoiced }, &[_]u32{ 10, 20, 30 }); // span 60
        build(&in.right, &[_]V{ .voiced, .silent }, &[_]u32{ 200, 200 }); // span 400
        var got = stereo_segment.StereoSegments{};
        try smooth_runs_stereo(&in, &got, 1000); // past the left's 60, past the right's 400
        if (got.left.len != 1 or got.left.items[0].verdict != .silent or got.left.items[0].count != 60) return error.SpanLeftWrong;
        if (got.right.len != 1 or got.right.items[0].verdict != .voiced or got.right.items[0].count != 400) return error.SpanRightWrong;
    }

    // 6. GROUNDED IN THE STEREO SEGMENTER — smoothing the runs ALES147 actually produces stays well-formed on both channels. A flickering left (a tick buried in a quiet
    //    stretch) and a clean right, smoothed with a modest min_run, hold each channel's structure.
    {
        var in = stereo_segment.StereoSegments{};
        build(&in.left, &[_]V{ .silent, .voiced, .silent, .voiced }, &[_]u32{ 40, 4, 40, 60 });
        build(&in.right, &[_]V{ .voiced, .silent }, &[_]u32{ 84, 60 });
        var got = stereo_segment.StereoSegments{};
        try smooth_runs_stereo(&in, &got, 8); // 8 > the 4-sample tick, <= the real runs
        if (got.left.len != 2 or got.left.items[0].verdict != .silent or got.left.items[0].count != 84) return error.GroundLeftWrong;
        if (got.left.items[1].verdict != .voiced or got.left.items[1].start != 84 or got.left.items[1].count != 60) return error.GroundLeftSpeech;
        if (!same(&got.right, &in.right)) return error.GroundRightChanged; // the right had no short run to absorb
        if (!well_smoothed(&got.left, &in.left, 8) or !well_smoothed(&got.right, &in.right, 8)) return error.GroundNotWell;
    }

    print("GREEN ales-stereo-smooth-runs: the RUN-LENGTH SMOOTHER CARRIED INTO STEREO, two independent hysteresis holds — ALES101 absorbs every run shorter than a " ++
        "minimum into what surrounds it, the smoothing hysteresis ALES95 named as a later rung, so a one-frame tick inside a pause no longer splits it. That is a mono " ++
        "rewrite. Carried into stereo it meets NO desync wall, unlike the silence edits — smooth_runs is READ-ONLY over the labels, rewriting a run-length sequence and " ++
        "touching no PCM, changing no clip length — so its honest stereo shape is the ANALYSIS-CORNER one ALES146 and ALES147 took: run the proven mono smoother on EACH " ++
        "channel independently, keeping the two results apart in a StereoSegments. The two channels flicker independently — ALES147 proved their segmentations differ in " ++
        "count and boundary — so they smooth independently: a tick on the left is absorbed by the left's neighbours, never the right's. The crux is the STEREO SMOOTH " ++
        "LAW: each channel's smoothed runs equal ALES101's mono smooth_runs on that channel, run for run, across a grid of min_run values and several per-channel input " ++
        "shapes — the rung invents no new rewrite and no new fault, composing the proven mono smoother twice, once per side, its whole content being that the two sides " ++
        "stay apart rather than one shared list. Proven too: the independence law (a left blip coalescing to one run while a clean right stays two, identical channels " ++
        "smoothing identically); the well-formed law (each output tiling the same span, coalesced, holding min_run past the first, per channel); and the degenerate law " ++
        "(min_run 0 the identity on both, an empty channel empty, a single run unchanged, a min_run past a channel's span coalescing it to one carrying its first " ++
        "verdict, independent of the other channel's span). Purely local, read-only — a rewrite of two run-length label sequences, NOT a transcript or a diarization; a " ++
        "causal left-to-right hold per channel, not the symmetric smoothing or adaptive floors a production VAD would add; no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no " ++
        "funds, no real speaker\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
